    "final_text": "The todo CLI is built, and all 48 tests pass (`npm test`). I didn't run a linter because the project has none configured. I also didn't commit anything.\n\nAll four commands work as specified:\n- `add \"<text>\"` appends a todo.\n- `list` prints `<id> [x] <text>` for done items and `<id> [ ] <text>` for pending ones.\n- `complete <id>` marks a todo done.\n- `delete <id>` removes it.\n\nTodos are stored in `todos.json` in the current directory, each with `id`, `text`, `completed` and `createdAt`. Setting `TODOS_FILE` points the CLI at a different file.\n\nEdge cases are handled:\n- **Missing arguments:** a missing or blank todo text, or a missing id, prints an error to stderr and exits 1.\n- **Invalid ids:** non-numeric, zero, negative and unknown ids also print an error and exit 1.\n- **Empty list:** `list` prints \"No todos yet.\"\n- **Corrupt `todos.json`:** the CLI reports the error and leaves the file untouched.\n\nSome behaviours you might not assume:\n- New ids are the highest existing id plus one. Deleting the newest todo frees its id for reuse, but the ids of the remaining todos never change.\n- Completing an already-completed todo succeeds silently.\n- There's no file locking, so if two commands write at once, the last write wins.\n\nThe README explains these decisions. I implemented only one id scheme (highest id plus one). CLAUDE.md asks for two approaches on algorithmic choices, but a counter or UUID would add stored state or break the integer-id requirement, so I explained that in the README instead.\n\nThe code is split into `index.js` (entry point), `src/cli.js`, `src/todos.js` and `src/storage.js`. The tests are in `test/` across three files: unit, edge cases and CLI including end-to-end runs through the real process.",
